<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Estates of Brittany</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Estates of Brittany were the representative assembly of the Duchy of Brittany, bringing together clergy, nobles and towns from the fifteenth century to vote taxes and advise the duke. After the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which they approved at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a> in 1532, they defended the province's privileges against the crown until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> swept them away in 1789–1790.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/estates-of-brittany.jpg" alt="Estates of Brittany" width="320" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-François Huguet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ouverture_des_Etats_de_Bretagne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Under the Ancien Régime the Estates of Brittany were one of the States Provincial of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. They brought together members of the high clergy, a large number of nobles and delegates from the 42 towns and cities of Brittany. The nobility dominated by sheer numbers: in 1788 the assembly included nearly 1,000 nobles, against fewer than 100 representatives of the other two orders. The duke who founded the Estates in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> also introduced the innovation of allowing the third estate to be represented.</p><p>Sessions were also social occasions. The Marquise de Sévigné attended meetings at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a> and Vitré between 1670 and 1690 and described the business as brief, since the Estates had only to ask what the king wanted, while the governor received over 40,000 écus, and there were pensions, gifts, road and town repairs, banquets, gaming, balls, plays three times a week and 400 pipes of wine. The banquets were paid for from the provincial budget, and disputes begun at the daily sittings were carried on through drinking, eating and card games.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On serious matters the Estates' interests were largely those of the Parliament of Brittany. The two bodies sometimes met at the same time and dealt with similar questions, and they cooperated in restricting royal power in the province and limiting the action of the crown's representatives there, chiefly the intendant and the commander in chief.</p><p>Taxation caused the sharpest conflicts. Brittany was exempt from the national salt tax and enjoyed other privileges, which the Estates defended against the royal state. In the late 17th century these disputes led to the uprising known as the Revolt of the papier timbré. Early in the 18th century the Estates resisted the king's centralising measures, and the conflict led to the Pontcallec Conspiracy, which the monarchy repressed severely.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Estates of Brittany” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The privileges guaranteed in 1532, the <b>Estates of Brittany</b> and the Parlement created in 1554 and fixed at Rennes in 1561 kept a strong provincial identity alive until the French Revolution, and Anne of Brittany remains a symbol of the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
